<div class="t6displayname wpproslider_t6_STRONG_5 wprev_preview_tcolor2_T6">Briauna Bergland</div>
<div class="wpproslider_t6_star_DIV"><span class="wprevpro_star_imgs_T6"><span class="starloc1 wprevpro_star_imgs wprevpro_star_imgsloc1"><span class="svgicons svg-wprsp-star"></span><span class="svgicons svg-wprsp-star"></span><span class="svgicons svg-wprsp-star"></span><span class="svgicons svg-wprsp-star"></span><span class="svgicons svg-wprsp-star"></span></span></span></div>
<div class="wpproslider_t6_SPAN_6 wprev_preview_tcolor2_T6"><span class="wprev_showdate_T6">9/05/2026</span></div>
</p></div>
</p></div>
<div class=" wpproslider_t6_DIV_4 rdmorepopbody">
<div class="wpproslider_t6_P_4 wprev_preview_tcolor1_T6">
						I wouldn’t trust any other vet with my axolotl, Bumble. We’ve been struggling with a bad appetite and other issues for two years now, but Dr. Bañuelos has gone above and beyond to look into how to help her each and every time we’ve come in, including doing surgery on her during our first visits. I’ve met very few vets who were willing to give as much genuine concern and care for traditional pets like cats and dogs (of which I have a few), never mind a 9” aquatic salamander. I’m so grateful to know that, when Bumble gets sick (which she does semi-frequently, as we’re on our 4-5 visit in two years), I have a vet who genuinely gives her all to help.
					</div>
</p></div>
